          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When a reference note names a root like K21, what do I look for to get to the specific code?",
          "answer": "The sources I searched do not give the subcodes under K21 or what their fourth and fifth characters mean. I can't list them without guessing, so you'll need the full ICD-10-CM tabular list for K20–K31 to confirm them.\n\nHere is what the sources do support.\n\n**Where K21 sits.** K21 is in the digestive chapter, in the block for diseases of the esophagus, stomach and duodenum (K20 to K31). Esophagitis comes first, then gastro-esophageal reflux disease, then the other esophageal categories (Chapter 11 summary, `coding-icd10-chapter-11-digestive`).\n\n**The two-step lookup.** Find the term in the Alphabetic Index, then check the code in the Tabular List. The index does not carry the instructional notes, and the Tabular List governs. Skipping the second step is a common cause of wrong codes (`coding-icd10-cm-code-structure-and-conventions`).\n\n**Why a three-character code may not be final.** Most categories are not valid at three characters, so the code must be carried to its full length. Some categories also use a placeholder \"X\" to hold a position open for a later character. The sources do not say whether K21 needs more characters. Check the Tabular List to see.\n\n**A pattern from another category.** Type 2 diabetes (E11) shows how fourth characters can work. There, they name the complication, and an ending in 9 marks diabetes without complications (`coding-type-2-diabetes-e11`). This is an example from a different category, not a description of K21.\n\n**Related points from the K21 coding note** (`coding-gerd-k21`):\n- Dysphagia is not routinely part of uncomplicated reflux disease. If it is documented, it is reported in addition.\n- Long-term drug therapy status and body mass index are coded from other chapters, not from K21.\n\nFor a worked K21 example with its subcodes, use the official ICD-10-CM tabular list for the current year, or a coding reference that reproduces it.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
